"## Summary\n\nNetlab follow-up 36: D-161 in the D-120 analyzer, with replays of every retained campaign and one live campaign.\n- **Session:** launched 03:16 EST on 2026-11-04 on #462's roadmap row, and delivered at 04:32.\n- **Model:** codex `gpt-6-astra`.\n- **Size:** 9 commits and 288 owned files, +380,423/−31.\n  - The code is 5 files in `tests/netlab/scripts/`, +553/−31: the classifier in `reload_analysis.py`, the new checked TCP parser `tcp_d161.py` and its tests, one reuse hook in `trace_split.py`, and one exact error assertion in `stream_evidence_test.py`.\n  - The rest is records: the dated note, the replays with their source pins, the gate receipts, and the full lab and agent-suite evidence.\n- **Checks:**\n  - merge-tree clean on current `main`; prepush-scan clean; owned-check passes.\n  - **The orchestrator reran these** on a scratch tree: current `main` (after #463) with secops 18, members 22 and this branch merged in. All pass:\n    - `make -f mk/netlab.mk netlab-lint netlab-unit netlab-integration netlab-build`, the 76 offline script tests included;\n    - `make -f mk/at-isolation.mk at-isolation-lint at-isolation-test`, since isolation's assembled run includes this lab;\n    - `go vet -tags netlab ./tests/netlab/...` and `go vet ./...`;\n    - `go test ./tests/handover/...`, and the committed-evidence tests (`TestCommitted*` in `tests/netlab`, `tests/e2e` and `tests/e2e/at/...`);\n    - `make go-lint`, gofmt and `git diff --check`;\n    - `docs/poc/G2-followup36-evidence/replay.py`, which reproduced all eight replays' verdicts and numbers and left the tree unchanged.\n- **Scope:** one live campaign (gate 2) and the agent lab, in the lane's own prefixes `fvpn-w1-netlab-f36` and `fvpn-w1-netlab-f36-node`, with no rerun and no reservation.\n  - It waited out members 22's assembled run and the orchestrator's reservation, outside any campaign.\n  - No production, consumer or dependency change. Zero owned Docker objects at the end.\n\n**The orchestrator's reading.**\n- **D-161 is in the pure classifier.**\n  - `classify_d120` stays pure, with the same verdict strings, exit status and existing fields. The new `classify_tcp` reads checked capture facts, never the captures.\n  - **A TCP timeout is attributed only if all of these hold:**\n    - every lost transmission of the stalled bytes entered the node and did not leave it;\n    - each entry is inside or on the boundary of exactly one verified guard bracket, with its upper bound and render→installed both ≤100 ms. These are the same rules UDP losses use, now shared in `guard_failures()`;\n    - the request was delivered, and its echo arrived in order on the same connection;\n    - no reset, close or second connection appears.\n  - **Anything else fails (iv),** with its failed conditions named: EOF, a reset, an unattributed timeout, or missing, incomplete or unparsed TCP captures.\n  - **(v)** counts UDP losses plus guard-attributed timeouts against the five-loss allowance. The synthetic proofs show 4 + 1 passing and 5 + 1 failing.\n  - **New fields,** in the analysis and the text verdict: TCP reads and timeouts, guard-attributed transmissions and timeouts, unattributed timeouts, and the longest TCP stall.\n- **Gate 2, the live campaign: AT10 PASS under D-120.**\n  - 64 TCP reads, zero TCP timeouts and zero UDP losses.\n  - 8 lost TCP transmissions, all at a guard bracket's boundary, each recovered by retransmission without a timeout. The longest stall is 217.7 ms, at sequence 12.\n  - **So D-161's timeout path did not run live.** The synthetic cases prove it.\n- **Replays of every retained campaign.** Source pins show 152 original artifacts byte-identical.\n  - **live2 to live5, and follow-up 35's gate 2,** pass as they did. Live3's longest stall is 613.0 ms, at sequence 47, with two boundary losses in generations 1069 and 1070, and still no timeout.\n  - **live1** replays as a pass under the splitter fix follow-up 35 made. Its original failure stays recorded.\n  - **F22-01 (isolation 22's run `7b0db5f2`) and F27-01/B1** fail, as before. They have no TCP captures, so their timeouts cannot be attributed.\n- **The orchestrator's code review.**\n  - **Exactly one bracket:** an entry that matches two brackets is `ambiguous` in `position()`, and fails (i).\n  - **A lost transmission** with no node entry, or with a node exit, fails attribution.\n  - **Connection errors,** a reset, a close or a second connection, fail (iv) even when no application read exposed them.\n  - **A deliberate tightening:** a campaign without combined TCP captures now fails as an evidence error, even with zero timeouts.\n    - Every current harness run retains them, since follow-up 35.\n    - Isolation's assembled run includes this same test, `TestAT10_AT13_ReloadRevokeBaseline` in `./tests/netlab` (`tests/e2e/at/isolation/run.py:57`), so it gets the same captures.\n  - **A missing UDP capture** is now an evidence error with a named reason, instead of an exception.\n- **Unchanged:** D-145's guard, the campaign's cadence, every other D-120 predicate, and the consumers: the node, the installer, isolation and the 13 agent-lab scenarios.\n- **Next:**\n  - **F22-01 stays open** until an isolation run with combined TCP captures is judged under D-161. That is isolation 23, armed on this merge.\n  - **Isolation 23 also takes the AT10 wording** this lane hands off, for its living procedure. The archived procedures keep theirs.\n"
